WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.410
Every summer, audiences across the globe are
treated to the ultimate beach body motivator:

00:00:05.410 --> 00:00:10.480
American Ninja Warrior, the obstacle course
competition that's taken the world by storm.

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:15.459
The show has a long and storied history, and
even super-fans might not realize just how

00:00:15.459 --> 00:00:20.130
far the show has come since it first aired
— and just how much goes into making it

00:00:20.130 --> 00:00:22.509
the epic production it is today.

00:00:22.509 --> 00:00:24.330
Humble beginnings

00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:28.349
Early seasons of American Ninja Warrior were
very, very different.

00:00:28.349 --> 00:00:33.699
It was initially known as American Ninja Challenge,
a video submission series in which contestants

00:00:33.699 --> 00:00:38.460
submitted videos of themselves on obstacle
courses, with the ultimate goal of being sent

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:43.739
to compete on a Japanese game show known as
"Ninja Warrior" in the States.

00:00:43.739 --> 00:00:48.469
(hype Japanese commentary)

00:00:48.469 --> 00:00:53.730
In those early seasons, contestants competed
on a qualifying course in Venice Beach, then

00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:58.460
the top 30 moved onto the finals, where they
entered a "boot camp" that whittled them down

00:00:58.460 --> 00:01:02.859
to just ten ninja finalists, who traveled
to Japan to compete.

00:01:02.859 --> 00:01:05.050
Primetime journey

00:01:05.050 --> 00:01:09.540
American Ninja Warrior didn't debut with a
plum primetime spot on NBC.

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:14.580
The show actually started out on G4, a network
within the company's cable group.

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:18.890
That channel doesn't exist anymore, but it
provided a home for American Ninja Warrior

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:22.150
for its first three increasingly popular seasons.

00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:27.390
According to host Matt Iseman, G4 went to
NBC during the third season and offered them

00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:33.180
the finale for free, saying: "'Just air it
on NBC to tell people G4 exists."

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:38.030
With little to no promotion, the obscure cable
show ended up winning the ratings for the

00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:42.810
night, leading to Ninja's permanent network
relocation in its fourth season.

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:44.810
Take a shot

00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:49.040
Have you ever wanted to run the American Ninja
Warrior course, but don't think you're ready

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:50.090
to apply?

00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:51.090
You're in luck.

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:55.330
The night before each city's run, a group
of volunteers is chosen for the chance to

00:01:55.330 --> 00:01:59.690
let producers know if they need to make any
last-minute adjustments to the course.

00:01:59.690 --> 00:02:03.500
There's no money involved, but they get the
chance to run the course and test their ninja

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:05.860
skills without all the pressure.

00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:09.789
Volunteers come from different walks of life,
including prospective contestants who sent

00:02:09.789 --> 00:02:14.349
in a tape but didn't make the cut, professional
stuntmen, and just regular folks.

00:02:14.349 --> 00:02:19.159
As a cool bonus, if you test out the course,
they let you stay and watch the taping.

00:02:19.159 --> 00:02:21.620
Hidden challenge

00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:26.930
Up until season five, American Ninja Warrior
contestants competed during the day, but since

00:02:26.930 --> 00:02:29.470
then, filming has taken place at night.

00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:34.319
Tapings in each city begin around 8 pm and
go into the wee hours of the morning, with

00:02:34.319 --> 00:02:37.810
some contestants doing their runs around 3
or 4 am.

00:02:37.810 --> 00:02:41.730
Even contestants who run earlier in the night
have to stick around that late if they want

00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:44.349
to find out if they made it into the finals.

00:02:44.349 --> 00:02:48.810
This is obviously pretty taxing on contestants,
adding a whole new layer of difficulty to

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:50.510
an already grueling show…

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:52.140
"Look at those forearms, Akbar."

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:54.410
"I wish I had forearms like that."

00:02:54.410 --> 00:02:57.019
"It looks like he's smuggling cantaloupes
in there!"

00:02:57.019 --> 00:02:59.150
Beyond the course

00:02:59.150 --> 00:03:02.840
Being a great athlete doesn't guarantee you
a spot on American Ninja Warrior.

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:06.739
While the show obviously puts quite a lot
of effort into finding contestants who are

00:03:06.739 --> 00:03:10.610
able to compete, there are a lot of other
factors that go into casting.

00:03:10.610 --> 00:03:15.389
To apply, contestants have to submit a long
online questionnaire, as well as a video of

00:03:15.389 --> 00:03:16.389
themselves.

00:03:16.389 --> 00:03:20.370
The video is where contestants have to really
let their personalities shine.

00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:23.640
That's when they try to convince the casting
directors that they won't just be able to

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:27.500
complete the course, but that they'll also
be fun to watch while they do it.

00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:30.400
"And this crowd is losing their minds!

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:35.790
And now she'll take on the even tougher back
half of the course!"

00:03:35.790 --> 00:03:40.209
As with most reality shows, Ninja's casting
directors are looking for contestants with

00:03:40.209 --> 00:03:44.959
interesting stories who will inspire viewers
both on and off the course.

00:03:44.959 --> 00:03:47.379
Full-time job

00:03:47.379 --> 00:03:50.999
Many ninjas have to quit their jobs if they
want to get serious about competing on the

00:03:50.999 --> 00:03:53.980
show, especially as the obstacles get more
difficult.

00:03:53.980 --> 00:03:58.609
Because being an elite-level ninja is basically
like being a pro athlete, contestants are

00:03:58.609 --> 00:04:03.139
constantly finding new ways to make a living
based on their athleticism, rather than through

00:04:03.139 --> 00:04:04.540
traditional jobs.

00:04:04.540 --> 00:04:08.930
This includes doing commercials and making
appearances at gyms and other events.

00:04:08.930 --> 00:04:12.939
However, some ninjas are still pushing for
more, with many believing that the future

00:04:12.939 --> 00:04:16.890
of the sport will come in compensation for
the athletes who appear on the show.

00:04:16.890 --> 00:04:21.760
The show has a $1 million prize — which
only one person has won as of the making of

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:27.070
this video — along with smaller cash prizes
for first, second, and third place in city

00:04:27.070 --> 00:04:28.530
finals courses.

00:04:28.530 --> 00:04:30.990
But there isn't compensation for all of the
athletes.

00:04:30.990 --> 00:04:35.170
"No one has reached this point until now!

00:04:35.170 --> 00:04:39.350
Isaac Caldiero will climb for a million dollars!"

00:04:39.350 --> 00:04:43.320
Some ninjas want the show to provide a base
compensation or for them to roll back their

00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:46.920
strict rules on athletes wearing sponsored
clothing on the course.

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:51.290
As the show continues to grow, and its competitors
get more famous, there will likely have to

00:04:51.290 --> 00:04:54.070
be some changes to keep the athletes coming
back.

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:57.880
Otherwise, the show's pool of potential contestants
could go extinct…

00:05:01.640 --> 00:05:04.140
"Down goes the T-Rex!"

00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:05.280
Thanks for watching!

